WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.800
Plan a forty-five to seventy-five second explanation.

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:11.559
Use three moments: the question and claim, the chart and evidence, and a limitation with a next observation.

3
00:00:11.759 --> 00:00:15.416
Draft about one hundred to one hundred forty words.

4
00:00:15.616 --> 00:00:21.564
Use the supplied fictional data or a non-sensitive classroom dataset, and label it honestly.

5
00:00:21.764 --> 00:00:24.439
Keep the chart’s unit labels visible.

6
00:00:24.639 --> 00:00:27.545
Choose a recording or slide-and-text route.

7
00:00:27.745 --> 00:00:34.745
Record narration in AudioSplat if helpful, then assemble the explanation in ClipSplat or VideoSplat.

8
00:00:34.945 --> 00:00:38.135
Use MediaSplat for a simple trim or join.

9
00:00:38.335 --> 00:00:42.283
Remove material that does not help the audience follow the reasoning.

10
00:00:42.483 --> 00:00:49.199
Keep the editable project so you can revise after feedback rather than starting again from the exported video.

11
00:00:49.399 --> 00:00:54.824
Ask a partner to state your claim, verify the two values and name the limitation.

12
00:00:55.024 --> 00:00:57.548
Test the explanation without sound.

13
00:00:57.748 --> 00:01:01.190
Correct captions and provide the transcript.

14
00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:05.883
Your deliverable is the explanation, source, chart and access check.

15
00:01:06.083 --> 00:01:12.565
If a partner cannot explain the reasoning after viewing it, revise the content before adjusting the colors or

16
00:01:12.765 --> 00:01:14.362
adding more effects.
